  "articleBody": "A stuck or decaying bathroom door is usually more than an annoyance. High humidity, plumbing splashes, and shifting framing can cause jambs to swell, rot, or pull away from the studs. Before hiring someone to patch the damage, make sure you know what to look for so the problem does not return in six months.\n\nWhat Can Change the Right Choice\nThe real question is whether you need a minor jamb patch or a full structural repair. Surface gouges and loose strike plates take only an hour or two to secure. However, if water has wicked upward from the floorboards into the bottom of the jamb, simple wood filler will fail. The installer must evaluate the rough framing, wall sill, and adjacent subfloor to make sure hidden rot is not spreading beneath the tile.\n\nQuestions to Ask Before You Decide\n- Can the existing jamb be partially spliced, or does the entire rough opening require replacement?\n- How will you verify that the framing and sill beneath the trim are completely dry before installing new wood?\n- Will you be using moisture-tolerant materials and sealing all cut edges to prevent future swelling?\n- How will you ensure the new jamb remains plumb so the door latches cleanly with an even reveal?\n\nWhen you are ready to fix your bathroom door frame properly, reach out to Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC by calling 303-505-0866 for an on-site assessment.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}